-- MAGNA CARTA (1297), 1297 c. 9 (25 Edw. 1), LAYER 1: THE NOUNS.
-- Types only: no rules, no #EVAL, no #ASSERT. Every other layer-1 module imports this one.
--
-- The text is the site's "traditional translation" (bracketed insertions and all), read from the extract
-- aep-Edw1cc1929-25-9-extract.md. It is not the official text, and nothing here says that any provision is,
-- or ever was, in force. See NOTES.md ("What this encoding does not claim").
--
-- Words the text uses and does not define are given as constructors or as plain inputs. A line marked
-- "gloss (not the text's)" is the encoder's plain-English aid and is no part of the law.

§ `Magna Carta (1297): the nouns`

-- The parties the text names as acting or as bound to act.
-- "We" and "Us" are the King who speaks; "our Heirs" are his Heirs; "all Men of this our Realm" are the third.
-- gloss (not the text's): the text does not say which King "We" is at each place; see the fork register, F-WE.
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the King`
    `an Heir of the King`
    `a Man of the Realm`

-- The places in the text where a speaker is named or where "We" speaks.
DECLARE Passage IS ONE OF
    `preamble paragraph 1`
    `preamble paragraph 2`
    `chapter 1`
    `chapter 9`
    `chapter 29`
    `the first unnumbered paragraph under the General Saving heading`
    `the second unnumbered paragraph under the General Saving heading`
    `the third unnumbered paragraph under the General Saving heading`

-- A speaker as the text styles him at the head of a paragraph: his name and the titles the text gives.
DECLARE Speaker HAS
    name IS A STRING
    titles IS A LIST OF STRING

-- The answer a rule gives when the text states something, or when it does not.
-- "not covered by the text" is NOT "no": it means the text is silent, and the asker's case is decided, if at all, elsewhere.
-- It plays the part of NOTHING. A rule answers it where the text is silent, lists without closing the list (no "only"), says "includes",
-- leaves a term undefined, or points to an authority outside the text, and the branch carries a comment saying which of those it is.
-- JUST FALSE or FALSE is answered only where the text says no in its own words (fork F-NOTCOVERED).
DECLARE Coverage IS ONE OF
    `covered by the text`
    `not covered by the text`

-- The classes of person the text names, on the one list that spans all its paragraphs.
-- gloss (not the text's): "Freeman", "Freeholder", "Templar", "Hospitaller", "Baron" and "Earl" are not defined by the
-- text; the asker says which one they are. A person may be of more than one class; the asker answers for one capacity at a time.
DECLARE Capacity IS ONE OF
    `an Archbishop`
    `a Bishop`
    `an Abbot`
    `a Prior`
    `an Earl`
    `a Baron`
    `a Freeman of the Realm`
    `a Templar`
    `a Hospitaller`
    `a Knight`
    `a Freeholder`
    `another Subject`
    `another Person`

-- How the reservation in the first unnumbered paragraph reaches a capacity: by being named in its list, or only as one
-- of "all Persons, as well Spiritual as Temporal".
DECLARE `How a capacity is reached` IS ONE OF
    `named in the list`
    `reached as one of all Persons`

-- The answer a rule gives where the text forbids something, or says nothing of it.
DECLARE Prohibition IS ONE OF
    `forbidden by the text`
    `not covered by the text`

-- A body, as chapter 1 speaks of one.
-- gloss (not the text's): "the Church of England" is the only body chapter 1 names.
DECLARE Body IS ONE OF
    `the Church of England`
    `another body`

-- How chapter 1's second sentence makes someone a holder of the Liberties under-written.
DECLARE `Holding under chapter 1` IS ONE OF
    `holds as a Freeman of the Realm`
    `holds as an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm`
    `not covered by chapter 1's second sentence`

-- Who chapter 9 speaks of.
-- gloss (not the text's): "Barons of the Five Ports" are persons, not places; they are listed with the places because the
-- sentence lists them together.
DECLARE Community IS ONE OF
    `the City of London`
    `another City`
    `a Borough`
    `a Town`
    `a Baron of the Five Ports`
    `another Port`
    `a community of another kind`

-- What chapter 9 says a community shall have, in the text's own words.
DECLARE `Chapter 9 entitlement` IS ONE OF
    `all the old Liberties and Customs which it hath been used to have`
    `all their Liberties and free Customs`
    `not covered by chapter 9`

-- A Liberty or a Custom, as chapter 9 and the General Saving speak of them.
DECLARE `Liberty or Custom` IS ONE OF
    `a Liberty`
    `a Custom`

-- What may be done to a Freeman, in the order chapter 29 lists it.
-- The first eight are said in the passive, with no actor named; the next two are done by "We".
-- "disseised of his Freehold, or Liberties, or free Customs" is read as three treatments, one per object (fork F-C29-DISSEISIN).
-- The last is the asker's "none of these": chapter 29 lists what it lists and says nothing of any other treatment.
DECLARE Treatment IS ONE OF
    `taken`
    `imprisoned`
    `disseised of his Freehold`
    `disseised of his Liberties`
    `disseised of his free Customs`
    `outlawed`
    `exiled`
    `any other wise destroyed`
    `passed upon`
    `condemned`
    `some other treatment`

-- What chapter 29 does with a Freeman and a treatment.
DECLARE `Chapter 29 outcome` IS ONE OF
    `forbidden by chapter 29`
    `not forbidden by chapter 29`
    `not covered by chapter 29`

-- The three things chapter 29's last sentence says "We" will not do with Justice or Right.
DECLARE Dealing IS ONE OF
    sell
    deny
    defer

-- What is dealt in. Chapter 29 names Justice and Right and nothing else.
DECLARE `Thing dealt in` IS ONE OF
    Justice
    Right
    `another thing`

-- The acts the regulative rules speak of.
-- gloss (not the text's): the two Booleans on the first two fields-bearing acts record whether the act is done "by lawful
-- judgment of his Peers" and whether it is done "by the Law of the Land"; the text defines neither phrase, so the asker states both.
DECLARE Act IS ONE OF
    `observe the Customs and Liberties`
    `procure or do a thing whereby the Liberties are infringed or broken`
    `pass upon a Freeman`
        HAS `by lawful judgment of his Peers` IS A BOOLEAN
            `by the Law of the Land` IS A BOOLEAN
    `condemn a Freeman`
        HAS `by lawful judgment of his Peers` IS A BOOLEAN
            `by the Law of the Land` IS A BOOLEAN
    `deal in Justice or Right`
        HAS how IS A Dealing
            what IS A `Thing dealt in`

-- The gifts and grants the second unnumbered paragraph ratifies, so far as this text shows them.
DECLARE `Gift or grant` IS ONE OF
    `the grant of the Liberties to the classes of preamble paragraph 2`
    `the grant of the Liberties to the Freemen of chapter 1`
    `the gift of the Fifteenth Part of Moveables`
    `the grant that neither We nor our Heirs shall procure an infringement`
    `a gift or grant this text does not show`

-- What the second unnumbered paragraph does with a gift or grant.
DECLARE Confirmation IS ONE OF
    `confirmed and made strong for the King and his Heirs perpetually, and renewed`
    `not covered by the second unnumbered paragraph`

-- What the first unnumbered paragraph says of a thing procured contrary to the premises.
-- The paragraph is introduced by "if", so when its condition fails the text does not make the thing void (fork F-SAVING-VOID, reconciled
-- 2026-10-30); the second value says that, in the pattern of `not commanded by the final clause` below. Both inputs of the rule that returns
-- an Effect are addressed by the text, so the type has no "not covered" value.
DECLARE Effect IS ONE OF
    `of no force nor effect`
    `not made void by the first unnumbered paragraph`

-- What the second unnumbered paragraph commands of an Article.
DECLARE Command IS ONE OF
    `commanded to be firmly observed from henceforth`
    `not commanded by the final clause`
